1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:06,201
People have told hero stories for as long as they have told stories.

2
00:00:06,561 --> 00:00:08,873
One of the oldest, the Epic of Gilgamesh,

3
00:00:08,873 --> 00:00:12,088
was written in Mesopotamia about four thousand years ago.

4
00:00:12,448 --> 00:00:13,859
The Greeks had Achilles,

5
00:00:13,859 --> 00:00:16,387
medieval Europe had its chivalric romances,

6
00:00:16,387 --> 00:00:18,209
and today we have epic fantasy,

7
00:00:18,209 --> 00:00:20,208
universe-spanning science fiction,

8
00:00:20,208 --> 00:00:24,206
and video games that let us act as the hero inside simulated worlds.

9
00:00:24,566 --> 00:00:28,291
The Swiss psychiatrist Carl Jung called the hero an archetype:

10
00:00:28,291 --> 00:00:30,274
a pattern that keeps reappearing.

11
00:00:30,634 --> 00:00:36,743
In 1949, the American mythologist Joseph Campbell published The Hero with a Thousand Faces.

12
00:00:37,103 --> 00:00:38,716
By faces, he meant people:

13
00:00:38,716 --> 00:00:42,377
each hero is another instance of the same idealised figure.

14
00:00:42,737 --> 00:00:46,297
The Legend of Zelda shows this in miniature: in each new era,

15
00:00:46,297 --> 00:00:48,748
a different Link takes up the hero's role.

16
00:00:49,108 --> 00:00:54,280
In Ocarina of Time, for twenty-eight years the highest-rated video game ever made,

17
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:56,172
he is called the Hero of Time.

18
00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:00,584
Every hero story is a kind of map for facing new and unknown problems.

19
00:01:00,944 --> 00:01:03,094
Campbell drew a map of those maps:

20
00:01:03,094 --> 00:01:04,992
a journey of seventeen stages,

21
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:06,573
from departure to return.

22
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:08,330
But he was mapping myths,

23
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:10,901
which are often symbolic, and he added stages,

24
00:01:10,901 --> 00:01:16,657
like meeting a goddess or atoning with a father, that many stories, and most real lives, never include.

25
00:01:17,017 --> 00:01:24,764
In Objective Knowledge, the philosopher Karl Popper described the pattern underneath far more simply: a problem, a tentative theory,

26
00:01:24,764 --> 00:01:27,405
the elimination of errors, and a new problem.

27
00:01:27,765 --> 00:01:30,781
It fits heroes in stories and real people alike,

28
00:01:30,781 --> 00:01:32,854
and it never ends where it began.

29
00:01:33,704 --> 00:01:38,976
Take away the costumes, and heroes across cultures share a handful of attitudes.

30
00:01:39,336 --> 00:01:41,986
They notice problems that others have learned to live with.

31
00:01:42,346 --> 00:01:45,447
They act despite fear, and keep going after failure.

32
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:49,299
They create new ideas instead of only repeating what they were taught.

33
00:01:49,659 --> 00:01:53,813
They treat their own mistakes as information to correct, not as shame to hide.

34
00:01:54,173 --> 00:01:57,162
They see evil as a problem too: something with causes,

35
00:01:57,162 --> 00:01:59,155
which can be understood and stopped.

36
00:01:59,515 --> 00:02:02,627
And they act on behalf of others, even when it costs them.

37
00:02:02,987 --> 00:02:06,477
These are the attitudes that make open-ended problem-solving possible.

38
00:02:06,837 --> 00:02:10,758
But why would cultures that never met keep inventing the same figure?

39
00:02:11,608 --> 00:02:13,953
Biology is full of convergent evolution.

40
00:02:14,313 --> 00:02:19,430
Distantly related species that face the same problem often reach similar solutions.

41
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:22,493
Insects, birds and bats each evolved wings,

42
00:02:22,493 --> 00:02:25,950
and octopuses and humans each evolved camera-like eyes.

43
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:29,400
This kind of convergence extends to cultures too:

44
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:33,814
writing was invented separately in Mesopotamia, China and Mesoamerica.

45
00:02:34,174 --> 00:02:37,383
Hero stories are another example of cultural convergence.

46
00:02:37,743 --> 00:02:41,903
Every culture tells of someone who faces a problem that plagues their society,

47
00:02:41,903 --> 00:02:43,769
where others have tried and failed,

48
00:02:43,769 --> 00:02:45,316
or shrunk from the challenge.

49
00:02:45,676 --> 00:02:50,992
The hero succeeds because they have an ingredient the others lacked: courage, wit,

50
00:02:50,992 --> 00:02:52,613
or a tenacious ingenuity.

51
00:02:53,463 --> 00:02:55,326
That is convergence in stories,

52
00:02:55,326 --> 00:02:58,330
but there is a second convergence, in real people.

53
00:02:58,690 --> 00:03:00,124
Across times and cultures,

54
00:03:00,124 --> 00:03:04,205
real individuals separately arrive at the same capacity to solve problems,

55
00:03:04,205 --> 00:03:08,342
even though most societies throughout history have reliably held them back.

56
00:03:08,702 --> 00:03:11,764
As Popper argued in Conjectures and Refutations,

57
00:03:11,764 --> 00:03:14,890
knowledge grows by finding and correcting errors.

58
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:17,433
A hero does this reliably, in themselves,

59
00:03:17,433 --> 00:03:19,563
and better than the culture around them,

60
00:03:19,563 --> 00:03:22,918
which makes them a revolutionary force at the level of culture.

61
00:03:23,278 --> 00:03:27,866
In Hayao Miyazaki's 1984 film Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind,

62
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:30,690
everyone fears a spreading toxic jungle.

63
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:33,563
Nausicaä refuses the dogma, studies it,

64
00:03:33,563 --> 00:03:36,655
and finds that it is cleaning the poisoned soil.

65
00:03:37,015 --> 00:03:38,709
Her judgement isn't perfect,

66
00:03:38,709 --> 00:03:42,340
but it is reliable enough to face problems no one predicted,

67
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:44,518
in familiar places and unknown ones.

68
00:03:45,368 --> 00:03:46,901
In The Beginning of Infinity,

69
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:50,126
the physicist David Deutsch argues that problems are soluble,

70
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:51,501
given the right knowledge.

71
00:03:51,861 --> 00:03:54,443
He goes further: every evil, he argues,

72
00:03:54,443 --> 00:03:56,496
comes from a lack of knowledge.

73
00:03:56,856 --> 00:04:00,059
So a hero treats all problems alike: a monster,

74
00:04:00,059 --> 00:04:03,603
a famine and a disease are all things to understand.

75
00:04:03,963 --> 00:04:05,460
That is the hero's reach,

76
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:10,190
and why a single error-correcting mind can overturn an entire culture's limits.

77
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:13,574
Because stories are maps of these people,

78
00:04:13,574 --> 00:04:16,726
the hero is often rediscovered, and often improved.

79
00:04:17,086 --> 00:04:18,882
Stories describe their heroes,

80
00:04:18,882 --> 00:04:21,157
try to explain what made them succeed,

81
00:04:21,157 --> 00:04:23,073
and criticise where they failed.

82
00:04:23,433 --> 00:04:25,175
Gilgamesh was a great warrior,

83
00:04:25,175 --> 00:04:28,194
but his epic opens with him oppressing his own city.

84
00:04:28,554 --> 00:04:33,084
Homer's Iliad names its subject in its first line: the rage of Achilles,

85
00:04:33,084 --> 00:04:34,846
and the suffering it caused.

86
00:04:35,206 --> 00:04:37,367
Achilles is the greatest fighter at Troy,

87
00:04:37,367 --> 00:04:40,423
yet his pride keeps him from battle while his friends die.

88
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:46,671
Many past heroes were heroic in one domain and bound by their culture's blind spots in others.

89
00:04:47,031 --> 00:04:50,469
Later stories keep what worked and correct what did not,

90
00:04:50,469 --> 00:04:52,924
so storytelling is error correction too.

91
00:04:53,774 --> 00:04:55,633
Campbell counted a thousand faces.

92
00:04:55,993 --> 00:04:59,082
For most of history, this kind of person was rare,

93
00:04:59,082 --> 00:05:02,850
reliably held back by static cultures that feared disruption.

94
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:08,679
But if a hero is anyone who corrects their own errors and treats every problem as soluble,

95
00:05:08,679 --> 00:05:10,138
that force has no limit.
